items.brief:
What is wrong today
growRealmTo(realm, id, population) is the ?grow path, and it is also how established and discovered towns get the buildings for the population they advertise. It leaves people homeless, and the homeless camp around the council (the 'plaza crowding' that was deferred earlier, '29 campers without housing'):
- The nearest Highland town at 600 people has housing 504 (96 campers).
- The nearest Suncoast town at 600 has housing 568.
- Many frontier towns at 1000 have 912 to 992 places.
- Growing a town twice (Willowbank to 100, then to 600) ends at 548.
The game promises that an established town shows enough buildings for the population it already advertises.

Required behaviour
The towns: the core towns 'alder', 'willow' and 'cairn', plus resolveGrowTarget(`culture:${c}`) for every culture c in CULTURE_IDS (13 distinct RealmIds in all). For each of them, on a fresh realm from createRealms(), after s = growRealmTo(realm, id, pop) for each pop in 128, 300, 600 and 1000:
- s.population is pop;
- settlementHousing(s) >= pop, and s.housing >= pop;
- no entry of s.residents has homeId 'camp'.

The same holds when a town is grown in steps on one realm, checked after the last step:
- 'willow' to 100, then to 600;
- 'alder' to 128, then to 1000;
- the Highland town (resolveGrowTarget('culture:highland')) to 300, then to 600;
- the Suncoast town (resolveGrowTarget('culture:suncoast')) to 150, then to 1000.

Existing behaviour stays intact:
- growRealmTo(realm, 'alder', NaN) still returns null and leaves the realm exactly as it was (JSON.stringify(realm) is unchanged);
- a crowded core valley loses no lot, and invalid saved ids are still dropped;
- existing tests that pass today must keep passing.
